<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sack of Rome (410)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/410">410 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 410 CE was the capture and plunder of the city by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> under Alaric. It was the first time in about eight centuries that Rome had fallen to a foreign enemy, and it caused deep shock across the Roman world. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> treat it as a symbol of the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>, which ended in 476 CE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was no longer the administrative capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>: Mediolanum had replaced it in 286 and Ravenna in 402, but it remained the eternal city and spiritual centre of the Empire. In 376 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> pushed many Thervings under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritigern">Fritigern</a> into the Eastern Roman Empire, and they rebelled and at Adrianople in 378 killed Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> died on 10 January 395 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> under Alaric considered their treaty ended and rose against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Alaric invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 401 and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stilicho">Stilicho</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pollentia">Pollentia</a> and Verona, and in 408, after Stilicho's death, he invaded again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Alaric besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in late 408. The city may have held as many as 800,000 people. He took control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> and cut its supplies, grain was rationed to a half and then a third, and starvation and disease spread. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a> refused every offer of terms. After a meeting arranged near Ravenna was broken by an attack from Sarus, an ally of Honorius, Alaric besieged Rome a third time.</p><p>On 24 August 410 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> through the Salarian Gate, opened, by one account, by treachery and, by another, by want of food, and pillaged the city for three days. They ransacked the mausolea of Augustus and Hadrian and scattered the ashes in the urns. The basilicas of Peter and Paul were spared. Some citizens were ransomed, others sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, raped or killed, and the emperor's sister Galla Placidia was taken captive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Alaric marched into southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with the city's wealth and Galla Placidia, but a storm wrecked the ships gathered to cross to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and he died of illness at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosenza">Consentia</a> in late 410. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> chose Ataulf as king, who married Galla Placidia in 414, and in 418 they settled in southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. Land taxes in the affected provinces fell to between a fifth and a ninth of their former value, and Bertrand Lançon estimates that the city's population fell from 800,000 in 408 to 500,000 by 419. The sack shocked people in both halves of the Empire. Theodosius II declared three days of mourning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, wrote that the city which had taken the whole world was itself taken.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sack of Rome (410)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
